1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,476
Many traders don't buy all at once.

2
00:00:02,526 --> 00:00:07,221
They place a ladder of orders below the price, so a dip fills them at better prices.

3
00:00:27,846 --> 00:00:30,450
The ladder helper sits right under the chart.

4
00:00:32,061 --> 00:00:34,857
Choose long or short, and the kind of market.

5
00:00:34,907 --> 00:00:38,215
Consolidating gives equal orders close to the price.

6
00:00:38,265 --> 00:00:42,213
Trending makes the nearest order smallest and the furthest largest.

7
00:00:42,684 --> 00:00:45,804
The orders are spaced by this coin's typical daily move,

8
00:00:45,824 --> 00:00:49,768
so a quiet coin gets a tight ladder and a wild one a wide ladder.

9
00:00:49,818 --> 00:00:52,166
The stop sits beyond the last order.

10
00:00:52,633 --> 00:00:54,554
Switch on Show on chart.

11
00:00:54,604 --> 00:00:58,295
Blue lines for the orders, thicker for a bigger share, red for the stop,

12
00:00:58,315 --> 00:01:01,347
and grey for your average price if every order fills.

13
00:01:01,802 --> 00:01:04,406
Or put the orders on Fibonacci levels.

14
00:01:04,456 --> 00:01:09,215
Draw a Fibonacci from the swing low to the high, then choose On Fibonacci levels.

15
00:01:09,265 --> 00:01:11,537
The orders move onto the retracement levels,

16
00:01:11,557 --> 00:01:14,045
and the stop goes just past the swing low.

17
00:01:14,095 --> 00:01:16,976
With no drawing, it measures the last swing for you.

18
00:01:19,867 --> 00:01:22,983
For the final entry, switch to five-minute candles.

19
00:01:23,033 --> 00:01:25,680
They cover the last seven days, for crypto.

20
00:01:35,631 --> 00:01:37,830
Switch the lines off when you're done.
